TEMPORARY diagnostic instrumentation for the cold-launch extension host OOM's blocking window -- not a real fix, remove once root-caused.

Replaces ios-exthost-activation-diagnostic.diff (reverted in the same commit
as this patch is added), which bracketed _doActivateExtension and was
verified via a real simulator-test.yml run to produce ZERO
"[iOS-DIAG activate]" output before the crash -- proving conclusively that
no extension's activate() is ever reached before the cold-launch OOM. That
rules out the "leading suspect" hypothesis recorded in CLAUDE.md from an
earlier session (typescript-language-features' TS compiler cold-load, or
similar slow-activation guess) and narrows the search from "which
extension's activate() is slow" to "what runs during the extension host's
own bootstrap, before initialize() ever gets far enough to activate
anything".

This patch brackets every step of that earlier bootstrap sequence instead,
across the four files that make it up, in call order:
- src/vs/workbench/api/node/extensionHostProcess.ts: the exthost's actual
  Node entry point (startExtensionHostProcess) -- createExtHostProtocol,
  connectToRenderer, and `new ExtensionHostMain(...)` construction.
- src/vs/workbench/api/common/extensionHostMain.ts: the ExtensionHostMain
  constructor itself (service collection setup, then handing off to
  IExtHostExtensionService#initialize() -- fire-and-forget, so the
  constructor returns before initialize() does any real async work).
- src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostExtensionService.ts: initialize()'s
  own two awaited steps (_beforeAlmostReadyToRunExtensions(),
  waitForInitializeCall()) and, further downstream, _startExtensionHost()/
  _handleEagerExtensions() (the '*' eager-activation path, kept
  instrumented in case bootstrap does get this far and something else
  blocks there instead).
- src/vs/workbench/api/node/extHostExtensionService.ts:
  _beforeAlmostReadyToRunExtensions() is the single richest candidate here
  -- it eagerly runs createApiFactoryAndRegisterActors() (builds the
  entire `vscode` namespace's API surface synchronously, hundreds of
  closures), NodeModuleRequireInterceptor(...).install() (require() hook
  installation), and connectProxyResolver(...) (which internally calls
  getExtensionPathIndex() -> fs.realpath() once per built-in extension
  with an entry point) -- all real, plausible sources of the previously
  observed multi-second block, all running before any activation event
  fires. Bracketed step by step to find out which one it actually is.

Every marker logs `Date.now()` (absolute epoch ms) rather than an elapsed
delta from a shared start time, deliberately -- these markers span three
separate files/modules with no convenient shared module-scope constant,
and absolute timestamps let post-hoc analysis compute gaps between any two
lines in the captured node-stdio.log regardless of which file emitted
them, the same way the original ~35-second gap was first noticed in
ios-exthost-worker-heap.diff's own heap-stats instrumentation.

console.error (not process.stderr.write), same reasoning as
ios-exthost-activation-diagnostic.diff already established: these files
are shared between the browser and Node extension host contexts, so
console.error's universal type avoids the DOM-vs-Node ambient timer-type
problem ios-exthost-heap-diagnostic.diff hit once already, and Node's
console implementation writes to the real process.stderr fd regardless of
which console.* method is called -- already confirmed piped into the
captured node-stdio.log by extensionHostConnection.ts's own
"<worker><stderr>" forwarding.

Purely additive, no behavior change -- only new console.error calls.
Meant to be removed (or converted into a real fix, if the culprit turns
out to be something fixable from vscode's own TypeScript source) once a
captured log shows which of these bracketed steps actually spans the
blocking window.
